#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""sf-snapraid: the root helper of Seraphina OS Harness's disk protection panel (DESIGN.md section 41;
docs/seraphina/BACKUPS_SNAPRAID.md section 8): the read-only reader, and the fallback while no SnapRAID Daemon is
installed. It protects nothing. With the daemon installed only info and disks run, and neither starts a SnapRAID
command but --version (every other verb answers daemon_runs); while a snapraid process of anyone else runs, the
verbs that start one wait (waiting). The version policy (section 8.2): 13.x and 12.x are read; a distribution build
that prints "v0" is 13.0 only when the package database owns that binary at 13.0-*; 14.x is read with care
(status, smart, probe and disks; guard and list refused); 15.x is read only through the daemon; two binaries of
different versions are refused, naming both.

The owner installs a copy at /usr/local/libexec/seraphina/sf-snapraid (root, 0755) and one sudoers line lets the
harness's user run it with no arguments at all. The request is one JSON line on standard input, at most 16 KiB:

    {"v": 1, "verb": "status"}

and the answer one JSON line on standard output: {"ok": true, ...}, or {"ok": false, "error": "<code>",
"words": "<a plain sentence>"}. Exit 0: it answered; 2: it refused; 1: its own failure. The answer, never the exit
status, carries SnapRAID's outcome. Two argument modes exist besides no arguments: `explain helper-config` and
`explain snapraid-config` print, for the owner running his own sudo, what the root-owned .new file he is about to
move into place allows and changes; `run <job id>` belongs to later phases' job units and is refused here.

The verbs of P1 (allow class "read"): info, status, smart, probe, disks, guard (the checks G1-G11 of the design's
section 4.2 as a report: "this sync would be held"; it never syncs), list, files, changes, job and log. Each
SnapRAID command runs as this program's own child, bounded by a timeout, with -c CONF and -l in a run folder
this program names; status, smart, probe, diff and list write nothing but SnapRAID's own lock. Every later verb
(sync, scrub, stop, release, drill, check, stat, restore, aside.*, repair.bad, restore.disk) is refused:
"not_in_this_version".

What it never does: no shell; no binary, config, log or state path from the caller or from any file the harness
stages (every one is written below); no SnapRAID command line it did not build from its verb table; no SnapRAID
test or expert option; no config it has not validated (SnapRAID turns a smartctl line into a shell command); never
writes the SnapRAID config; never writes, renames or deletes anything on the array; never follows a symbolic link
on the array (the one stat it makes there, for the guard's "replaced by a similar file", walks with O_NOFOLLOW);
never reads file contents on the array; never sends SIGKILL (a run past its time limit gets SIGINT, then SIGTERM);
never returns a file name except from files, changes and log, a page at a time.

Standard library only; never imported by the harness at run time (the tests load it by path).
"""

